<title>DUBAI: Abu Dhabi-based ARBIFT lifts 1996 profits.</title>
<headline>Abu Dhabi-based ARBIFT lifts 1996 profits.</headline>
<dateline>DUBAI 1997-01-20</dateline>
<text>
<p>Year ended December 31, 1996</p>
<p>		    (million dirhams unless stated)</p>
<p> Net profit				  161.539	vs  143.439</p>
<p> Profit available for appropriation  173.315	vs  153.214</p>
<p> Net interest income		     102.120	vs   83.748</p>
<p> Commission, other income		171.034	vs  174.615</p>
<p> Provisions				   55.0	  vs   60.0</p>
<p> Proposed total dividend		  57.0	  vs   42.75</p>
<p>Full name: Arab Bank for Investment and Foreign Trade.</p>
<p>NOTE: The Abu Dhabi-based bank is owned by the United Arab Emirates (42.5 percent), Libyan Arab Foreign Bank (42.5 percent) amd Algeria's Banque Exterieure d'Algerie (15 percent).</p>
<p>It has been banned from doing business with U.S. banks since 1992 because the United States considers Libya a major shareholder. The bank says blocked funds held with banks in the United States totalled 261 million dirhams in 1996 from 249 million in 1995.</p>
<p>The bank proposed a dividend of 10 percent, or 2,000 dirhams, a share for 1996, versus 7.5 percent in 1995.</p>
<p>-- Dubai newsroom +9714-626990</p>
</text>
